==German==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|goh|de}} {{term|eih|lang=goh}}, from {{proto|Germanic|aiks|lang=de}}; akin to {{etyl|nl|-}} {{term|eik}}, {{etyl|en|-}} {{term|oak}}, {{etyl|sv|-}} {{term|ek}}. Ultimately derived from {{proto|Indo-European|eiǵ-|lang=de}}.

''Further Indo-European cognates:'' Latin {{term|aesculus||Durmast oak|lang=la}}, Latvian {{term|ozols|lang=lv}}, Lithuanian {{term|ąžuolas|lang=lt}}, Albanian {{term|enjë||juniper, yew|lang=sq}}

